# Input
```vue
<!-- should generate diagnostics -->
<template>
  <div v-if="foo">foo</div>
  <div v-else v-for="x in xs">{{ x }}</div>

  <div v-if="foo">foo</div>
  <div v-else-if="bar" v-for="x in xs">{{ x }}</div>

  <div v-if="foo">foo</div>
  <div v-for="x in xs" v-else>{{ x }}</div>

  <div v-if="foo">foo</div>
  <TodoItem v-for="todo in todos" v-else-if="bar" :todo="todo" />
</template>

```

# Diagnostics
```
invalidElse.vue:4:15 lint/correctness/noVueVIfWithVFor ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

  × Using v-else and v-for on the same element is discouraged.
  
    2 │ <template>
    3 │   <div v-if="foo">foo</div>
  > 4 │   <div v-else v-for="x in xs">{{ x }}</div>
      │               ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    5 │ 
    6 │   <div v-if="foo">foo</div>
  
  i Move this v-else to a wrapper element, such as a <template>.
  
    2 │ <template>
    3 │   <div v-if="foo">foo</div>
  > 4 │   <div v-else v-for="x in xs">{{ x }}</div>
      │        ^^^^^^
    5 │ 
    6 │   <div v-if="foo">foo</div>
  
  i The v-else is checked before the v-for loop runs, which is hard to tell when reading the template.
  

```

